WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.810
A staple of daytime TV since 1972, The Price
is Right is the longest-running game show

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.920
in American television history.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:13.550
Hosted by the iconic Bob Barker for more than
35 years and 7,000 episodes, it's continued

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:17.570
to thrive after Barker's retirement under
the stewardship of comedian Drew Carey.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.419
But how much do you actually know about this
television institution?

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:24.479
Here's a look at the untold truth of the Price
is Right.

00:00:24.479 --> 00:00:25.720
Let's Make a Deal

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:31.009
When The Price is Right debuted in 1972, it
was actually titled The New Price is Right,

00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:33.399
a title it kept for a little under a year.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:38.640
That's because it was loosely based on a primetime
game show that had aired as early as 1956,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.820
where contestants won small prizes by guessing
how much household items cost.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.449
But by the '70s, the original concept was
deemed too boring.

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:51.600
Instead, CBS told producer Mark Goodson that
if he wanted to revive the Price is Right,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.720
he should look at their hit game show Let's
Make a Deal for inspiration.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.739
He did, borrowing a slew of gimmicks, like
pulling people out of the studio audience

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:03.050
to be contestants, using lights and sound
effects to get the crowd hyped, and having

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:07.390
the contestants play lots of different little
games, like the cultural phenomenon Cliff

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:08.390
Hangers.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.220
"There's no way that microwave costs more
than 300 bucks.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.220
Stop here.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.960
Don't listen to that fat tourist, he doesn't
know how much a microwave costs!"

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.400
"I can't find dad!"

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.480
"Oh, God, no!

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.020
Oh, God, no!

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.320
Where is he?!"

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.400
"Hans!

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:24.420
Hans!

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:24.960
Where are you?!

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.120
Oh, my God."

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.120
"Dad?"

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.500
The best of the best

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.020
Every game show has a different process for
contestant selection.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:36.180
Jeopardy!, for example, is quite rigorous,
putting prospective guests through multiple

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.049
written tests, an interview, and a live test
version of the game against other hopefuls.

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:42.930
The Price is Right is far more immediate.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:47.940
On taping days, contestants/studio audience
members line up for as long as four hours

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.170
outside Bob Barker Studio at CBS Television
City in Los Angeles.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:56.759
Then, ten at a time, they're herded in front
of a producer who asks them each one question,

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:00.920
where they've got to show off a sparkling
personality and lots of energy.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.820
Only about 100 people or so get picked to
sit in the studio audience, and the lucky

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.750
contestants find out they've been selected
when the announcer calls their name.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:13.690
"How about if we call Katie Hudson, COME ON
DOWN!

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.150
YOU'RE the next contestant on The Price Is
Right!"

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.770
Showcase Showdown showdown

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:25.860
In 2008, a huge Price is Right fan named Terry
Kniess was picked to "come on down" to Contestant's

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:26.860
Row.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.020
He made it all the way to the Showcase Showdown,
where he did something that nobody in the

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:35.550
show's history had done, before or since:
He guessed the exact price of his prize package.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:40.210
Taping stopped for almost an hour, as the
producers and host Drew Carey were sure Kneiss

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:41.210
had cheated.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.210
But he hadn't.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:46.330
Instead, he had studied the show for years,
and realized that many prizes were repeated.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:51.160
So he memorized the prices of all the prizes
and added them up in his head during his appearance.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.361
As a result, he won both showcase prize pools,
and the Price is Right changed their prize

00:02:55.361 --> 00:02:58.440
structure to include more variance in price
structure.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.420
"You bid $23,743.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:03.620
Actual retail price?

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.200
$23,743, you got it right on the nose."

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.480
Replacing a legend

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.200
Finding a replacement for Barker wasn't easy.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:16.200
The search was so difficult, in fact, that
he even offered to delay his retirement if

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.260
a new host couldn't be found in time.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:24.390
Numerous stars secretly recorded mock episodes
as host, including George Hamilton, Ian Ziering,

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:29.540
Mario Lopez, John O'Hurley, Antiques Roadshow
host Mark Walberg, and former Double Dare

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.150
host Marc Summers.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:36.680
Summers was Bob Barker's first choice as replacement,
but after CBS chairman Les Moonves saw Carey

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:41.070
hosting the prime time game show Power of
10, he offered Carey the gig.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.900
Too bad Adam Sandler didn't audition!

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.280
"The price is wrong, bitch!"

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.120
A BRAND NEW CAR!

00:03:55.130 --> 00:04:00.070
As Benjamin Franklin once famously said, only
two things in life are certain: Plinko and

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:01.070
taxes.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:04.040
And California takes their game show taxes
very seriously.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:08.110
They actually treat game show winnings as
income, and contestants are required to pay

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:11.400
taxes on the spot before they can take their
prizes home.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.040
Luckily, many contestants also win money on
the show in addition to prizes, so it's common

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:18.260
for them to pay taxes using their prize money.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.600
And there's a bunch of red tape around that
brand new car, too.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.800
Winners don't drive home with their sweet
ride.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:28.550
Instead, the show sends them to a local dealership
to coordinate delivery of the vehicle at a

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:32.650
dealership near the contestant's home - assuming
they have already paid all those taxes and

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:33.940
registration fees first.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.400
"It's your BRAND NEW CAR!"

